  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ be the number of cups of lemon juice and $y$ be the number of glasses of lemonade.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that $x=4$ cups of lemon juice are required for $y=18$ glasses of lemonade. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{18}{4}=\\frac{18÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that Jane can make $y=12$ glasses of lemonade. So, we substitute $y=12$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{9}{2}x \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\frac{9}{2},$ which is $\\frac{2}{9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}\\times \\frac{9}{2}x & =\\frac{2}{9}\\times 12 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\times 12}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24÷3}{9÷3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jane requires $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ cups of lemon juice to make $12$ glasses of lemonade.",
